  <h2 class="mt-12">The Best Section: Yangdi to Xingping</h2>
  <p>This 20 km stretch is the Li River's greatest hits. It passes the Nine Horse Fresco Hill, the Yellow Cloth Reflection, and ends near the viewpoint on the 20 RMB note. If you only do one thing in the region, do this section.</p>
  <p>Bamboo rafts run from Yangdi to Jiuma Horse Hill (the most scenic 9 km). Motor boats run the full Xingping route. Both are excellent — the bamboo raft is more atmospheric; the motor boat gives you a longer experience with a roof for sun/rain.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">Ticket Prices (2026)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Full cruise (Mopanshan to Yangshuo):</strong> ~360 RMB per person. Includes a basic lunch on board. Departs around 9 AM daily.</li>
    <li><strong>Bamboo raft (Yangdi to Jiuma):</strong> ~120 RMB per person. 4 passengers per raft. Buy tickets at the official pier — not from touts.</li>
    <li><strong>Bamboo raft (Xingping to Jiuma):</strong> ~250 RMB for a round trip. Shorter but convenient if you're already in Xingping.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-stone-400">Prices are for 2026. Bring cash — some ticket offices don't accept foreign cards. See our <a href="/guilin-yangshuo-trip-cost" class="text-accent underline">full cost breakdown</a> for a complete trip budget.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">How to Book</h2>
  <p>Book through your hotel (they'll add a small commission but handle logistics), Trip.com (English, reliable), or at the official pier ticket office. Do not book from street touts or unlicensed "guides" who approach you at the bus station — they'll charge double and may take you to a different, less scenic river section.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">Getting to the Pier</h2>
  <h3>From Guilin</h3>
  <p>The full cruise departs from <strong>Mopanshan Pier</strong> (also called Zhujiang Pier), about 40 minutes from Guilin city center. Most hotels can arrange a shuttle for 30-50 RMB. Public bus #9 from Guilin Railway Station goes to the pier area, but you'll need a taxi for the last 2 km — total about 2 hours. Taxi direct: 80-100 RMB, 40 minutes.</p>
  <h3>From Yangshuo</h3>
  <p>The cruise only runs <strong>one direction</strong> — Guilin to Yangshuo. If you're staying in Yangshuo, take a <a href="/guilin-to-yangshuo" class="text-accent underline">bus to Guilin</a> (1.5 hours, 25 RMB), then taxi to the pier. Alternatively, do a bamboo raft section near Yangshuo (Xingping to Jiuma) instead of the full cruise.</p>
  <h3>Combining With Your Stay</h3>
  <p>The classic approach: check out of your Guilin hotel, take the cruise with your luggage, and check into your Yangshuo hotel on arrival. Most boats have luggage storage. If you're doing a <a href="/guilin-yangshuo-itinerary-3-day" class="text-accent underline">3-day itinerary</a>, the cruise is your Day 2 transport — it's the experience and the transit in one.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">What to Expect On Board</h2>
  <h3>Motor Boat Experience</h3>
  <p>Large two-deck boats with indoor seating, an open upper deck for photos, and a basic Chinese lunch included. The scenery commentary is in Mandarin — don't expect English narration. Most boats have 100-150 passengers. The upper deck fills up fast, so board early if you want a good spot for photos. Bring snacks and water — the included lunch is edible but forgettable.</p>
  <h3>Bamboo Raft Experience</h3>
  <p>A motorized bamboo raft (4 passengers) with a canopy for shade. You sit close to the water — it's a more intimate, quieter experience than the big boat. The raft operator may point out named rock formations. Life jackets are provided and required. You'll get sprayed occasionally in rapids sections — keep your phone in a waterproof bag.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">Photography Tips</h2>
  <h3>Best Light</h3>
  <p>The Li River looks best in <strong>morning light</strong> (7-9 AM) and <strong>late afternoon</strong> (4-6 PM). Midday sun flattens the karst peaks and creates harsh shadows. The classic misty-mountain shots you see in travel photos are usually taken at dawn in spring or autumn. For the best photo spots beyond the cruise, see our <a href="/guilin-yangshuo-photo-spots" class="text-accent underline">photo spots guide</a>.</p>
  <h3>Gear Tips</h3>
  <p>A wide-angle lens (24-35mm on full frame) captures the sweeping river-and-peaks compositions. A telephoto (70-200mm) isolates individual peaks in the mist. A polarizing filter cuts water reflections. And bring a lens cloth — river spray is constant on the bamboo rafts.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">What to Bring</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Water:</strong> At least 1L per person. Boats sell drinks at inflated prices.</li>
    <li><strong>Sun protection:</strong> Sunscreen, hat, sunglasses. The river reflects UV — you'll burn faster than you expect.</li>
    <li><strong>Rain jacket:</strong> Even on sunny days, afternoon showers are common in summer.</li>
    <li><strong>Waterproof phone bag:</strong> Essential on bamboo rafts. Available at the pier for 10-20 RMB.</li>
    <li><strong>Cash:</strong> For snacks, drinks, and tips. The boat crew will pass around a tip box at the end — 20-50 RMB is standard if service was decent.</li>
    <li><strong>Camera with strap:</strong> Dropping your phone or camera into the Li River is not a recoverable situation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-12">When to Go</h2>
  <h3>Best Months</h3>
  <p>April, May, September, and October offer the best combination of comfortable weather and clear skies. See our <a href="/best-time-to-visit-guilin" class="text-accent underline">month-by-month weather guide</a> for details.</p>
  <h3>Water Levels by Season</h3>
  <p>The Li River is <strong>rain-fed</strong> — water levels fluctuate significantly by season, affecting cruise quality and availability:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spring (Mar–May):</strong> Highest water levels after the spring rains. Best flow, deepest channel, and the bamboo rafts ride smooth and fast. The peaks are lush green and wildflowers bloom along the banks. Ideal conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Summer (Jun–Aug):</strong> Water levels drop in July–August but afternoon storms can raise them quickly. The landscape is at its greenest. Hot and humid on the water — bring more water than you think you need.</li>
    <li><strong>Autumn (Sep–Nov):</strong> Moderate water levels with the clearest skies. The best light for photography. By November, levels begin dropping and some smaller channels may be too shallow for motor boats.</li>
    <li><strong>Winter (Dec–Feb):</strong> Lowest water levels. Full-length motor boat cruises may be shortened or re-routed. Bamboo rafts are still operating on most sections. The river is quieter and the misty landscape creates dramatic, moody photos. If water is too low, the cruise may be substituted with a bus to Yangdi and a shorter bamboo raft section — still scenic, but not the full experience.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Time of Day</h3>
  <p>The full cruise departs around 9 AM and arrives in Yangshuo around 1 PM. Morning departures get the best light for photography. Afternoon bamboo raft departures have smaller crowds. Avoid the 8 AM rush — that's when all the tour groups arrive.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-12">Combine With These Activities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><a href="/xingping-ancient-town" class="text-accent underline">Xingping Ancient Town</a>:</strong> The cruise ends near Xingping — stay an extra hour to walk the old street and see the 20 RMB banknote viewpoint.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/yulong-river-bamboo-raft" class="text-accent underline">Yulong River Bamboo Raft</a>:</strong> If you loved the Li River bamboo raft, the Yulong is a quieter, more scenic tributary. Do both — they're different experiences.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/yangshuo-cycling-routes" class="text-accent underline">Yangshuo Cycling</a>:</strong> After the cruise drops you in Yangshuo, rent a bike and explore the countryside. The 10km classic route starts from town.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/guilin-food" class="text-accent underline">Beer Fish Dinner</a>:</strong> End your cruise day with Yangshuo's signature dish at a riverside restaurant.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-red-50 p-6">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-700">Common Scams</p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
      <li><strong>Fake river sections:</strong> Some operators will take you to a nearby tributary, not the actual Li River. Confirm "Yangdi to Xingping" explicitly.</li>
      <li><strong>Inflated prices:</strong> If someone quotes more than 400 RMB for a bamboo raft, walk away.</li>
      <li><strong>"The cruise is cancelled" scam:</strong> Touts at the bus station will tell you the official boats aren't running and offer an "alternative" — it's a lie. Walk to the pier and check yourself.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
